 Run locally

1. Clone or download the repository.
2. Open frontend.html in a browser (or use the VS Code *Live Server* extension).
3. Test at 100% browser zoom (Ctrl + 0). Several pixel values and scroll triggers depend on window size, and testing at a different zoom hides real behaviour (see bug E1).

 How it works

The one rule the whole project follows: JavaScript does not animate anything. It adds and removes class names, and CSS does the visual work.

When the page loads, script.js runs once from top to bottom, then waits for timers and scroll events:

1. Setup (runs immediately): selects the elements with document.querySelector, builds the videos array and picks a random index with Math.floor(Math.random() * videos.length).
2. Observers created (callbacks stored, not run): two IntersectionObservers wait for the second section to appear.
3. Loader counter: setInterval runs every 20ms, increasing i and updating the percentage text and the progress bar width.
4. At 100%: the loader gets the class hide (CSS slides it up), the website gets show, the body loses loading so scrolling unlocks, the random video loads, and one second later the video plays and the title gets reveal-title.
5. On scroll: when enough of the second section is visible, the first observer shows the glass panel, reveals the "Hero" letters, and doubles the first strip's images. The second observer reveals both strips and doubles the second strip's images.

Every reveal in the project uses the same three parts:

1. Start state: a hidden look in CSS (for example opacity: 0 and transform: translateY(70px)).
2. Animation or transition: how it gets from hidden to visible.
3. Trigger: a class that JavaScript adds at the right moment.

